<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lower Nubia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> is the stretch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> valley between the First and Second Cataracts, in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>. Home to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/a-group-culture">A-Group culture</a>, it was repeatedly conquered by Egypt, which ruled it as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wawat">Wawat</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viceroy-of-kush">Viceroy of Kush</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>. In late antiquity the Blemmyes and the Nobadae contested it, and it became the heart of the Christian kingdom of Nobadia. Much of it was flooded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-nasser">Lake Nasser</a> after the building of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aswan-high-dam">Aswan High Dam</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lower-nubia.jpg" alt="Lower Nubia" width="320" height="393" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: UNESCO · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:List_of_monuments_at_risk_in_the_1960_UNESCO_Courier_-_UNESCO_documents_regarding_the_International_Campaign_to_Save_the_Monuments_of_Nubia.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the 1550s the Ottoman Empire, having conquered Egypt, pushed south into <b>Lower Nubia</b>, and a clash with Ottoman forces at Hannik, near the Third Cataract, probably in the later sixteenth century, fixed the long-term frontier between the two powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/funj-sultanate">Funj Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>From there a reduced Christian kingdom, known in its documents as Dotawo, survived in <b>Lower Nubia</b> into the late fifteenth century, while Islam and Arabic gradually spread through the former kingdom.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The main field of expansion was <b>Lower Nubia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/middle-kingdom-of-egypt">Middle Kingdom of Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>It was preceded by the pre-Kerma culture, which developed between about 3500 and 2500 BCE and formed statelets and small kingdoms, probably larger than those of the A-Group culture in <b>Lower Nubia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/culture/kerma-culture">Kerma culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The A-Group people lived in <b>Lower Nubia</b>, between the First and Second Cataracts, from about 3800 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/a-group-chiefdom-of-qustul">A-Group chiefdom of Qustul</a></li><li><blockquote>They were organised in tribes under kings and phylarchs, and in the 5th century held parts of <b>Lower Nubia</b>, with Talmis (Kalabsha) as a centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/blemmyes">Blemmyes</a></li></ul></section>
